The Civilization Codec: A Conditional Framework for Closure-Fidelity Encoding and Distributed Epistemic Integrity

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Scope and Standing Hypotheses

The object of study is a deductive corpus $(S_O, \mathrm{Cn})$ consisting of a finite set of atoms $S_O$ and a Tarskian closure operator $\mathrm{Cn}:2^{S_O}\to 2^{S_O}$ (extensive, monotone, idempotent) induced by a proof system with rules of the form $(T; b)$, $T\subseteq S_O$ finite, $b\in S_O$. A corpus realization is a subset $k\subseteq S_O$. The closure-fidelity encoding problem asks: what is the minimum rate at which realizations $k$ can be encoded so that a decoder recovers $\mathrm{Cn}(k)$ exactly?

The source model is stated once and held fixed throughout. A sender has access to a shared knowledge base $(S_O,\mathrm{Cn})$ and emits a stream of statement reports, each of the form "atom $s\in k$", for a realization $k$ drawn i.i.d. from a distribution $P_O$ on $2^{S_O}$. The receiver knows $\mathrm{Cn}$ but not $k$. The rate $R(0)$ of Xu (2026) is the minimum rate at which this report stream can be compressed so that $\mathrm{Cn}(k)$ is recoverable exactly. The framework below does not price the transmission of $\mathrm{Cn}$ itself; where the title refers to "civilizational knowledge," that phrase denotes the report stream $\{k_t\}$ over a shared deductive environment, not the environment.

Two standing external anchors are used and not re-proved: the rate characterization of Xu (2026, arXiv:2604.15698v3), which we cite by §-number rather than restate; and the terminal-SCC algorithm of Allamigeon (2014, arXiv:1112.1444). Every other result is proved here under its explicit hypotheses.

