Part I — Mathematical Foundations

1. A Cellular-Sheaf Codec

1.1 The sheaf and its coboundary. Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite graph, taken to be the nerve of a cover of the context poset, i.e. vertices are knowledge contexts and edges are overlaps. A cellular sheaf $\mathcal{F}$ on $G$ assigns to each vertex $v\in V$ a vector space $\mathcal{F}(v)$ with norm $\|\cdot\|_v$, to each edge $e=uv\in E$ a vector space $\mathcal{F}(e)$ with norm $\|\cdot\|_e$, and to each incidence $v\unlhd e$ a linear restriction map $\rho_{v\unlhd e}:\mathcal{F}(v)\to\mathcal{F}(e)$. The space of 0-cochains is $C^0=\bigoplus_{v\in V}\mathcal{F}(v)$ and of 1-cochains $C^1=\bigoplus_{e\in E}\mathcal{F}(e)$. The coboundary is the linear map $\delta:C^0\to C^1$ with
(\delta x)_e \;=\; \rho_{u\unlhd e}(x_u) - \rho_{v\unlhd e}(x_v),\qquad e=uv.


Cohomology is $H^0=\ker\delta$ (global sections, i.e. agreements across all edges) and $H^1=\mathrm{coker}\,\delta=C^1/\mathrm{im}\,\delta$.

This replaces the ad hoc defect of the earlier draft. The codec defect is
d_{\mathcal{L}}(x):=\|\delta x\|_1 \;=\; \sum_{e=uv}\bigl\|\rho_{u\unlhd e}(x_u)-\rho_{v\unlhd e}(x_v)\bigr\|_e .


The raw fidelity between two section assignments $x$ and $x'$ over the same cover is
\widetilde\phi(x,x')\;=\;1-\frac{d_{\mathcal{L}}(x-x')}{\|N(x)\|+\|N(x')\|},


with $N$ a normalization operator making numerator and denominator commensurable; the clamped fidelity is $\phi=\max(0,\widetilde\phi)$. When the denominator vanishes, the fidelity is recorded as the undefined symbol $\bot$ in the provenance bundle; we do not identify $\bot$ with $0$ or with $1$.

1.2 Interpreting $H^1$ correctly. The gluing axiom of a sheaf is automatic: compatible local sections on a cover glue uniquely. Therefore "gluing failure" cannot occur in an honest cellular sheaf, and the earlier conjecture's "iff" was ill-posed. The correct reading is the following.

Proposition 1.1 (Obstruction, one-directional). Let $\mathcal{F}$ be a cellular sheaf on $G$. Then:
(i) $H^0=\ker\delta$ is the space of exact agreement states on $G$;
(ii) a section class $[\,y\,]\in H^1$ is nonzero if and only if the 1-cochain $y$ is not in the image of $\delta$, i.e. there is no vertex re-encoding $x\in C^0$ with $\delta x=y$.

Proof. (i) is definitional. (ii): $[y]=0$ in $C^1/\mathrm{im}\,\delta$ iff $y\in\mathrm{im}\,\delta$. $\square$

The operational content is the contrapositive: if no vertex re-encoding can eliminate an edge-disagreement pattern $y$, then $[y]\neq 0$ in $H^1$. We do not claim the converse direction "gluing fails $\Rightarrow$ $H^1\neq 0$" because, as noted, gluing never fails; we claim only that the residual of a disagreement, after optimal vertex re-encoding, is measured by $H^1$. This is the sense in which $H^1$ can be used as a diagnostic of irreducible translation defect.

1.3 Fidelity range.

Proposition 1.2 (Range). For $\|N(x)\|+\|N(x')\|>0$, $\phi(x,x')\in[0,1]$, with $\phi=1$ iff $\delta(x-x')=0$ and $\phi=0$ iff $\|\delta(x-x')\|_1\ge \|N(x)\|+\|N(x')\|$. For vanishing denominator, $\phi$ is recorded as $\bot$.

Proof. $\|\delta(x-x')\|_1\ge 0$ by nonnegativity of norms; the upper bound and the two equivalences are direct from the definition; the zero case is by the convention. $\square$

Proposition 1.2 is a normalization statement, not a deep result. Its role is to fix the fidelity scale so that the FEL's QC layer (Part II) has a well-posed scalar; it does not by itself contribute to the rate characterization.

1.4 The sheaf Laplacian. The matrix representation of $\delta$ with respect to an orthonormal basis of $C^1$ is $\delta\in\mathbb{R}^{|E|\times |V|}$ with blocks $\pm\rho_{u\unlhd e}$. The sheaf Laplacian is $\mathcal{L}=\delta^\top\delta:C^0\to C^0$, positive semidefinite with $\ker\mathcal{L}=H^0$. The optimal edge-defect residual at a fixed section $x$ is $\|H\,\delta x\|$ where $H=I-\delta\delta^\dagger$ is the orthogonal projector onto $(\mathrm{im}\,\delta)^\perp$, i.e. the harmonic representative of the class $[\delta x]$ in $H^1$. This is the diagnostic that Part II uses: it isolates the irreducible translation defect from the part that any vertex re-encoding can eliminate.

