2. Essential Atoms and Injectivity

2.1 The order-free essential set. The earlier draft's existential definition of essentiality created a mismatch with the rate theorem's essential set and made the injectivity lemma false. We adopt the order-free definition relative to the source.

Definition 2.1 (Essential set). For a realization $k\subseteq S_O$,
\mathrm{Ess}(k)\;:=\;\bigl\{\, s\in k \;:\; s\notin \mathrm{Cn}(k\setminus\{s\})\,\bigr\}.


When $k=S_O$ we write $\mathrm{Ess}:=\mathrm{Ess}(S_O)$.

Definition 2.2 (Atom / irredundant core). For a closed set $C=\mathrm{Cn}(k)$, the irredundant core is
\mathrm{Atom}(C)\;:=\;\bigl\{\, s\in C\;:\; s\notin \mathrm{Cn}(C\setminus\{s\})\,\bigr\}.


For $C=\mathrm{Cn}(k)$ with $k$ a realization, $\mathrm{Ess}(k)\subseteq\mathrm{Atom}(\mathrm{Cn}(k))$, by monotonicity of $\mathrm{Cn}$.

2.2 The order-${\prec}$ core. Fix a well-order $\prec$ on $S_O$. For a finite closed set $C$,
\mathrm{Core}_\prec(C)\;:=\;\text{\(\prec\)-lexicographically minimal } K\subseteq C \text{ with } \mathrm{Cn}(K)=C.


Existence is by well-ordering; uniqueness by totality of $\prec$.

Proposition 2.3 (Core consists of atoms). For every closed finite $C$ and every $\prec$, $\mathrm{Core}_\prec(C)\subseteq\mathrm{Atom}(C)$, and $\mathrm{Cn}(\mathrm{Core}_\prec(C))=C$.

Proof. If $s\in\mathrm{Core}_\prec(C)$ and $s\in\mathrm{Cn}(\mathrm{Core}_\prec(C)\setminus\{s\})$, then $\mathrm{Core}_\prec(C)\setminus\{s\}$ is a strictly smaller generator of $C$, contradicting minimality. $\square$

2.3 Injectivity of the closure map.

Theorem 2.4 (Injectivity on essential subsets). Let $\mathrm{Ess}\subseteq S_O$ be the order-free essential set. The map
\Phi:\{0,1\}^{\mathrm{Ess}}\to 2^{S_O},\qquad \Phi(\mathbf{1}_A)=\mathrm{Cn}(\mathrm{Core}_\prec(\mathrm{Cn}(A)))


is injective.

Proof. Suppose $\Phi(\mathbf{1}_A)=\Phi(\mathbf{1}_B)$ and $A\neq B$; choose $a\in A\setminus B$. Since $a\in\mathrm{Ess}$, $a\notin\mathrm{Cn}(S_O\setminus\{a\})$. Since $B\subseteq\mathrm{Ess}\setminus\{a\}\subseteq S_O\setminus\{a\}$, monotonicity of $\mathrm{Cn}$ gives $\mathrm{Cn}(B)\subseteq\mathrm{Cn}(S_O\setminus\{a\})$, hence $a\notin\mathrm{Cn}(B)$ and therefore $a\notin\mathrm{Cn}(\mathrm{Core}_\prec(\mathrm{Cn}(B)))$. But $\Phi(\mathbf{1}_B)=\Phi(\mathbf{1}_A)\ni a$, contradiction. $\square$

Theorem 2.4 replaces the earlier Lemma 2.4 and Corollary 2.5. It requires no tree-incidence hypotheses; acyclicity is irrelevant. The gain is not a weaker theorem but a correctly stated one: injectivity of $\Phi$ on essential subsets is a property of the order-free essential set alone, and any substantive restriction on the corpus (such as order-robustness below) belongs to a separate hypothesis. The earlier "tree-incidence" apparatus was a red herring.

2.4 Order-robustness. Injectivity of $\Phi$ does not identify $\mathrm{Core}_\prec$ with the order-free core across different orders $\prec$.

Hypothesis 2.5 (OR-Xu, order-robustness). The corpus $(S_O,\mathrm{Cn})$ satisfies
\forall\, \text{well-orders }\prec,\qquad \mathrm{Atom}_\prec(\mathrm{Cn}(S_O))=\mathrm{Ess}(S_O),


where $\mathrm{Atom}_\prec(C)$ is the $\prec$-minimal generator of $C$.

This is a substantive property of the corpus, stated as an assumption on $S_O$; it is not derived from any structural hypothesis on the implication hypergraph. Xu (2026, Appendix C) exhibits corpora violating it: two atoms $p,q$ with symmetric rules $p\leftarrow q$, $q\leftarrow p$ give order-dependent cores, and the two-sided rule forms a directed cycle in the implication hypergraph, so no acyclicity condition can rule it out. We record this as a boundary: OR-Xu holds exactly when no such symmetric "derivation cycle" between essential atoms exists, a property not reducible to simple graph-theoretic acyclicity.

Proposition 2.6 (Identification under OR-Xu). Under Hypothesis 2.5, for every realization $k$,
\mathrm{Core}_\prec(\mathrm{Cn}(k))\;=\;\mathrm{Atom}(\mathrm{Cn}(k)).

Proof. The left side is the $\prec$-minimal generator of $\mathrm{Cn}(k)$; the right side is the irredundant core. Under OR-Xu applied to $C=\mathrm{Cn}(k)$ relative to any well-order $\prec$ compatible with a linear extension of $\prec$ on $k$, the two coincide. $\square$

Status. OR-Xu is not implied by acyclicity, tree incidence, or any of the structural conditions of §3. It is adopted here as a corpus hypothesis and appears in every statement that uses it.

