3. Confusable Atoms and Hypergraph Reachability

3.1 Implication hypergraph. The implication hypergraph is
\mathcal{H}\;=\;\bigl(S_O,\; E_{\mathcal{H}}\bigr),\qquad (a_1,\dots,a_m; b)\in E_{\mathcal{H}} \iff b\in \mathrm{Cn}(\{a_1,\dots,a_m\})\setminus\textstyle\bigcup_j \mathrm{Cn}(\{a_j\}).

3.2 All-tail reachability. For $u,v\in S_O$, $v$ is reachable from $u$, written $u\rightsquigarrow v$, iff either $u=v$ or there is a hyperedge $e=(T(e);H(e))$ with $v\in H(e)$ and every $t\in T(e)$ satisfies $u\rightsquigarrow t$. This is the standard all-tail definition (Allamigeon 2014).

3.3 Confusable blocks. Two atoms $a,b\in S_O$ are confusable if there exist directed paths $a\rightsquigarrow b$ and $b\rightsquigarrow a$ under $\rightsquigarrow$. The confusable blocks are the maximal equivalence classes under the transitive closure of confusability — equivalently, the strongly connected components of $\mathcal{H}$ under $\rightsquigarrow$.

Proposition 3.1 (Confusable blocks are all SCCs). The confusable blocks partition $S_O$ into the strongly connected components of $\mathcal{H}$ under $\rightsquigarrow$.

Proof. Mutual reachability is an equivalence relation on $S_O$; its classes are exactly the SCCs by definition. $\square$

This is the corrected structural claim. The earlier draft's Algorithm 4 used Allamigeon's terminal-SCC algorithm, which computes only sink SCCs; that is not the confusable-block partition.

3.4 Terminal SCCs are not a partition into confusable blocks. In the digraph $a\leftrightarrow b,\ b\to c$, the confusable blocks are $\{a,b\}$ and $\{c\}$; the terminal SCC is $\{c\}$ alone. Hence the terminal-SCC algorithm cannot be used as a substitute for all-SCC computation without changing the definition of confusable block.

Two honest choices are available.

Choice A. Compute all SCCs. Allamigeon (2014, abstract) proves a superlinear lower bound on the size of the transitive reduction of the reachability relation and a linear-time reduction from "finding all minimal sets" to all-SCC, indicating that all-SCC is strictly harder than terminal-SCC. Under Choice A the algorithm is correct but no longer almost-linear.

Choice B. Redefine the confusable diagnostic to use terminal SCCs, which Allamigeon computes in $O(\mathsf{size}(\mathcal{H})\,\alpha(n))$. This is legitimate only if the rate characterization does not depend on non-terminal SCCs — which, by §4 below, it does not, because the confusable-core rate is governed by Xu's hypergraph entropy, not by any local block decomposition.

The framework adopts Choice B for the algorithmic layer, with the explicit statement that the object computed is the terminal confusable diagnostic (TCD), not the full confusable-block partition. §3.3 remains as the definition of "confusable" and §4 below does not use the full partition.

