4. Zero-Distortion Rate Under Closure Fidelity

4.1 Xu's rate theorem. The rate characterization is external. We state the two independent hypotheses it rests on, keep them distinct, and do not conflate them.

Definition 4.1 (Realization source). Let $S_O$ be a fixed finite corpus with closure $\mathrm{Cn}$, and let $P_O$ be a distribution on $2^{S_O}$ generating i.i.d. realizations $k_1,k_2,\dots$. For a canonical scan order of $S_O$, let $A=\mathrm{Atom}(\mathrm{Cn}(k))$ and $\pi_A=P_O(\cdot\mid A)$.

Hypothesis 4.2 (Disjointness). For each closed set $C$ in the support of $P_O$, the collection of subsets $K\subseteq C$ with $\mathrm{Cn}(K)=C$ consists of sets that are pairwise disjoint on atoms of $A$; equivalently, no atom of $A$ belongs to two distinct minimal generators of $C$.

Hypothesis 4.3 (Order-robustness). Hypothesis 2.5.

Theorem 4.4 (Xu 2026, §III). Under Hypotheses 4.1 and 4.2, the minimum zero-distortion rate under closure fidelity in the non-confusable regime is
R(0)\;=\;P_A\,H(\pi_A).


Under Hypotheses 4.1–4.3 the characterization interpolates between classical symbolwise compression and unconstrained deductive compression. In the general confusable regime (Hypothesis 4.2 relaxed), $R(0)$ is the hypergraph entropy induced by jointly realizable core subsets, with a reduction to Körner graph entropy under a natural pairwise realisability condition.

Citation. Xu, arXiv:2604.15698v3, §III (Theorem 3.1) and §III-C (confusable-core characterization).

Remark 4.5 (Hypothesis separation). The rate formula $R(0)=P_A H(\pi_A)$ rests on Hypothesis 4.2 alone. Order-robustness (Hypothesis 4.3) is additional: it licenses the interpolation interpretation, not the formula. The earlier draft treated order-robustness as the missing hypothesis on which the formula depends; that was a misplacement. The formula is intrinsic to the closure-fidelity problem, and order-robustness is a separate, optional structural condition on the corpus.

This separation is the primary correction of the present framework and is recorded as a standing classification throughout.

4.6 The Körner connection. In the confusable regime, define the confusability graph $G_c$ on $A$ by $a\sim_{G_c}b$ iff $\{a,b\}$ is a jointly realizable atom pair (i.e. there exists a closed $C$ in the support with $a,b\in\mathrm{Atom}(C)$ and a realization whose core contains both). Under the natural pairwise realisability condition (Xu 2026, §III-C), the confusable-core rate is the Körner graph entropy of $\pi_A$ with respect to $G_c$,
R(0)\;=\;H_{G_c}(\pi_A)\;:=\;\min_{X\sim \pi_A,\,X\perp Y}\ I(X;Y)\big/\dots,


where the min ranges over joint distributions on independent pairs $(X,Y)$ with $X\sim\pi_A$ and $Y$ ranging over independent sets of $G_c$. We state this to make the connection explicit; the evaluation is external.

4.7 The one-sided comparison. Xu's Corollary 3.2 in the non-confusable regime gives the additive decomposition
H(P_O)\;=\;h_b(P_A)\;+\;P_A\,H(\pi_A)\;+\;P_J\,H(\pi_J),


with $h_b$ the binary entropy of the core-indicator and $J$ the "junk" mass of non-core atoms. The inequality $R(0)\le H(\mathbf{1}_{Ess})$ of the earlier draft is therefore only an interpretation of the first two summands and is not a standalone corollary. It is deleted.

