Part II — Architecture

5. The FEL: State Algebra

5.1 Why a product alone is not sufficient. Let
\mathrm{FEL}\;:=\;\bigl(OR_{add},\; RW_{epistemic}\bigr)


where $OR_{add}$ is an add-wins observed-remove set and $RW_{epistemic}$ is a remove-wins set (each is a join-semilattice under its respective join). The product of two join-semilattices is a join-semilattice componentwise. However, "the visible set of $n$" is a predicate on the product, not a component, and the earlier draft left this predicate unspecified. Without an explicit arbitration rule, Propositions 5.12 and 5.13 (below) do not compose.

5.2 Explicit arbitration. Each $OR_{add}$ element is a pair $(n,\, T_n)$ with $T_n\subseteq\mathrm{Tag}$ a finite set of add-tags. Each $RW_{epistemic}$ element is a pair $(n,\, g_{\mathrm{del}}(n))\in\mathbb{N}$ recording the deletion generation. The visible set is
\mathrm{Vis}(S)\;=\;\bigl\{\, n \;:\; \exists t\in T_n,\ \neg\exists g_{\mathrm{del}}\in RW_{epistemic},\ g_{\mathrm{add}}(t)\le g_{\mathrm{del}}(n) \,\bigr\},


where $g_{\mathrm{add}}(t)$ is the generation stamped on the add-tag $t$ at insertion. Equivalently: $n$ is visible iff there is a live add-tag not dominated by the current epistemic deletion generation. Visibility is a function of the state, not a component; it is not monotone under join, but it is stable under join, i.e. $S=S'$ implies $\mathrm{Vis}(S)=\mathrm{Vis}(S')$.

Proposition 5.1 (Product semilattice). The componentwise join
(OR_1,RW_1)\sqcup(OR_2,RW_2)\;=\;\bigl(OR_1\sqcup_{OR}OR_2,\; RW_1\sqcup_{RW}RW_2\bigr)


is commutative, associative, and idempotent.

Proof. Componentwise join of join-semilattices. $\square$

5.3 Delivery and convergence.

Hypothesis 5.2 (RD). Every delta produced at a replica is eventually delivered to every other replica at least once; duplicate delivery is absorbed by idempotence; no delta is permanently lost.

Corollary 5.3 (Strong eventual consistency). Under (RD) and Proposition 5.1, replicas that have received the same set of deltas have identical state, and therefore identical visible set.

Proof. Merge is a join under Proposition 5.1; state is the join of received deltas; equal delta sets give equal joins; visibility is a function of state. $\square$

