6. Garbage Collection

The earlier draft's garbage-collection propositions assumed that a tombstone's continued presence was available at GC time, which is exactly what GC removes. The corrected statements below make the persistence requirements explicit.

6.1 Causal stability, precisely. A removal delta $\delta_{\mathrm{rm}}(n)$ is causally stable at replica $i$ if every replica has acknowledged receipt of $\delta_{\mathrm{rm}}(n)$ and every delta in its causal past, and has relinquished any tag $t$ it previously held for $n$ that is covered by the removal. The second clause is not standard causal stability; it is added because, under (RD) alone, a replica may continue to hold and re-emit a stale tag.

Hypothesis 6.1 (NR, no-reintroduction). Once a removal delta $\delta_{\mathrm{rm}}(n)$ is causally stable at every replica and the removal's tombstone has been GC'd at replica $i$, no replica can subsequently emit a fresh delta carrying any tag $t$ covered by $\delta_{\mathrm{rm}}(n)$.

Hypothesis 6.2 (WM, watermark). Alternatively to (NR): after GC of the tombstone for $t$, replica $i$ retains a persistent tag watermark $W_i\subseteq\mathrm{Tag}$ such that any future delta carrying $t\in W_i$ is absorbed as a duplicate and does not contribute to $\mathrm{Vis}$.

Proposition 6.3 (OR stale-delta safety, corrected). Suppose (RD) and either (NR) or (WM) hold. Let $\delta_{\mathrm{rm}}(n)$ be causally stable at every replica, and let $t_1$ be a tag covered by $\delta_{\mathrm{rm}}(n)$. Then GC of $t_1$ at any replica $i$ cannot resurrect $n$ via a late delivery of a duplicate delta carrying $t_1$.

Proof. Under (NR): after GC at $i$, the hypothesis guarantees no replica emits a delta carrying $t_1$. Any duplicate already in transit arrives before the causal-stability barrier; it is either absorbed into the OR set and then removed, or (if it arrives after the barrier) absorbed by the still-present tombstone prior to GC. Under (WM): a late duplicate carrying $t_1$ meets the watermark $W_i$ and is absorbed; visibility is unaffected. $\square$

Remark 6.4 (What the proposition does not claim). Proposition 6.3 concerns duplicate late delivery of a tag $t_1$ already known at the removing replica. A genuinely fresh add with a new tag $t_2$ (not covered by $\delta_{\mathrm{rm}}(n)$) is handled by the add-wins semantics of the OR component and is not suppressed; suppressing fresh adds would contradict add-wins semantics.

6.2 Generation suppression.

Hypothesis 6.5 (GF, generation floor). For every $RW$ element $n$, the deletion generation $g_{\mathrm{del}}(n)$ persists in a monotone floor $F_i(n)$ at each replica $i$, even after the corresponding tombstone is GC'd.

Proposition 6.6 (RW generation suppression). Under (RD) and (GF), an add with generation $g\le F_i(n)$ is suppressed by replica $i$ that has merged the removal of generation $F_i(n)$, and GC of the stable tombstone does not weaken the suppression.

Proof. The floor $F_i(n)$ is monotone under merge and preserved by GC. An add with generation $g\le F_i(n)$ is dominated in the RW join by the removal generation, and its membership in $\mathrm{Vis}$ is excluded by the definition of $\mathrm{Vis}$. $\square$

6.3 The hybrid's combined guarantee. Under (RD), (NR) or (WM), and (GF), the hybrid state $\mathrm{FEL}=(OR,RW)$ with visibility rule §5.2 delivers: stale OR deltas are absorbed (§6.1); stale RW adds with generation $\le$ floor are suppressed (§6.2); fresh OR adds survive under add-wins semantics unless dominated by an epistemic deletion in the RW component.

