7. Controversy: Epoch Lattice

The earlier draft's four-element stance lattice with join $=\max$ made the laundering-prevention theorem nearly tautological. We replace it with an epoch lattice that models re-opening as a new epoch, so that "resolved" is not an absorbing state.

7.1 The stance lattice. $\mathcal{S}=\{0<\mathrm{challenged}<\mathrm{supported}<\mathrm{resolved}\}$ with join $=\max$ and meet $=\min$; finite, distributive.

7.2 The epoch lattice. $\mathcal{E}=\mathbb{N}$ with join $=\max$ and meet $=\min$. A position is a pair $(e,s)\in\mathcal{E}\times\mathcal{S}$. The product $\mathcal{E}\times\mathcal{S}$ is a distributive lattice under componentwise operations.

Definition 7.1 (Supersession). A supersession delta is a delta $\delta_{\mathrm{sup}}$ carrying $(e+1,\,s_{\mathrm{sup}})$ with $s_{\mathrm{sup}}\in\mathcal{S}$ and an evidence witness $\varepsilon$ satisfying $\varepsilon>\varepsilon_{\mathrm{threshold}}$. We treat evidence as a scalar; the threshold is part of the protocol.

Rule 7.2 (Laundering prohibition). A position $(e,s)$ derived from a descendant of a resolution $(e_\ast,\mathrm{resolved})$ may not lower the stance below $\mathrm{resolved}$ at epoch $\le e_\ast$ without a supersession delta at epoch $e_\ast+1$.

7.3 Laundering prevention.

Proposition 7.3. Under Rule 7.2 and the product lattice $\mathcal{E}\times\mathcal{S}$, no sequence of derivations and joins of positions can lower the visible stance of an epoch-$e_\ast$ resolution without a supersession delta at epoch $e_\ast+1$.

Proof. Let $P_\ast=(e_\ast,\mathrm{resolved})$ be a resolution. The join of $P_\ast$ with any position $(e,s)$ is $(\max(e,e_\ast),\max(s,\mathrm{resolved}))=(e',\mathrm{resolved})$ for $e'\ge e_\ast$, so the stance coordinate cannot drop through join. Any position with $e<e_\ast$ is joined to $(\cdot,\mathrm{resolved})$ and yields a resolved stance. Any position with $e>e_\ast$ is a new epoch and, by Rule 7.2, requires a supersession delta; without it, the position is inadmissible in the derivation. Hence the stance of $P_\ast$ survives all admissible joins without a supersession delta. $\square$

Remark 7.4 (Why the product matters). On the flat four-element lattice, the proof collapsed because $\max$ is an absorbing operation at the top. The product with $\mathcal{E}$ gives the lattice a second dimension in which "resolved at epoch $e$" is not the same as "resolved at epoch $e+1$," so a re-opening requires an evidence-increasing operation and is not absorbed by max. The theorem is therefore substantive: it excludes laundering precisely because the epoch dimension is preserved.

