Part III — Algorithms

Algorithm 1 (Coboundary evaluation and fidelity).
Input: cellular sheaf $(\mathcal{F},\rho)$ on $G=(V,E)$, section $x\in C^0$.
Output: $\delta x\in C^1$, $d_{\mathcal{L}}(x)$, $\phi$.
For each $e=uv$, compute $(\delta x)_e=\rho_{u\unlhd e}(x_u)-\rho_{v\unlhd e}(x_v)$; accumulate $\|\delta x\|_1$; compute $\phi$ by §1.3; record $\bot$ when the denominator vanishes. Complexity: $O(\sum_{e}\dim\mathcal{F}(e))$.

Algorithm 2 (Sheaf Laplacian residual).
Input: $\delta$ in sparse form, $x\in C^0$.
Output: $\|H\,\delta x\|$, the harmonic representative of the class $[\delta x]$ in $H^1$.
Compute $\mathcal{L}=\delta^\top\delta$; solve $\mathcal{L}\,y=\delta^\top\delta x$ for $y$; return $\delta x-\delta y$. Complexity: $O((|V|+|E|)\log(|V|+|E|))$ via sparse Cholesky; $O(|V|^3)$ via dense.

Algorithm 3 (Core extraction).
Input: closed finite $C$, closure oracle $\mathrm{Cn}$, well-order $\prec$.
Output: $\mathrm{Core}_\prec(C)$.
Order atoms of $C$ by $\prec$; initialize $K:=C$; for each $a\in C$ in increasing $\prec$-order, if $\mathrm{Cn}(K\setminus\{a\})=C$, set $K:=K\setminus\{a\}$. Return $K$. Complexity: $O(|C|)$ closure calls, hence linear in $|C|\cdot T_{\mathrm{Cn}}$.

Algorithm 4 (Terminal confusable diagnostic).
Input: implication hypergraph $\mathcal{H}=(S_O,E_{\mathcal{H}})$.
Output: the terminal SCCs of $\mathcal{H}$ under $\rightsquigarrow$.
Invoke Allamigeon's terminal-SCC algorithm. Complexity: $O(\mathsf{size}(\mathcal{H})\,\alpha(|S_O|))$ where $\mathsf{size}(\mathcal{H})=|S_O|+\sum_{e}(|T(e)|+|H(e)|)$.
Correctness caveat: the output is a terminal SCC set, not the confusable-block partition of §3.3. The framework uses terminal SCCs as the algorithmic diagnostic (Choice B of §3.4); the full partition requires an all-SCC algorithm, for which Allamigeon (2014) establishes a superlinear lower bound.

Algorithm 5 (LWW join with antisymmetry).
Input: LWW-Map entries $\ell_i=(\mathrm{target},t_i,r_i)$ on a key $k$.
Output: per-key maximum.
Order entries by $(t,r)$ lexicographic. For antisymmetry, require either the per-key uniqueness invariant $(t_i,r_i)\neq(t_j,r_j)$ for $i\neq j$, or, when $(t,r)$ collides, a value tie-breaker on $\mathrm{val}(\ell)$. Complexity: $O(|E|)$.

Algorithm 6 (FEL merge).
Input: FEL states $S_1=(OR_1,RW_1)$, $S_2=(OR_2,RW_2)$.
Output: $S_1\sqcup S_2$.
Return $(OR_1\sqcup_{OR}OR_2,\,RW_1\sqcup_{RW}RW_2)$ where $RW$ join takes $\max$ on generations and $OR$ join takes union of tag sets, filtered by (WM) if present. Compute visibility by §5.2. Complexity: linear in state size.

Algorithm 7 (GC safety check).
Input: replica state, candidate tombstone $t_1$ for node $n$, delivery log.
Output: safe-to-GC or unsafe.
Check (i) $\delta_{\mathrm{rm}}(n)$ causally stable at every replica; (ii) either (NR) or (WM) holds for $t_1$; (iii) if $RW$, (GF) holds for $g_{\mathrm{del}}(n)$. Return safe iff all hold. Complexity: $O(|\mathrm{Replica}| + |T_{t_1}|)$.

