Part IV — Experimental Protocol (Calibration)

The following protocol is specified so that the theoretical results can be empirically interpreted. It is not a substitute for proof, and success in it does not promote conditional results to unconditional ones.

E1. Choose a Datalog fragment with materialized EDB/IDB stores, as in Xu §IV; report $|S_O|$, $|E_{\mathcal{H}}|$, max arity, terminal SCC count.

E2. Compute $P_A$ and $H(\pi_A)$ under the canonical scan order and compare with $H(\mathbf{1}_{\mathrm{Ess}})$. Report the gap $H(\mathbf{1}_{\mathrm{Ess}})-P_A H(\pi_A)$ as a function of confusability, using Xu's synthetic instance first as a sanity check before moving to a real corpus.

E3. Order-robustness test: for at least three well-orders $\prec$ (lexicographic, topological, random), compute $\mathrm{Atom}_\prec(\mathrm{Cn}(S_O))$ and compare with $\mathrm{Ess}$; report whether (OR-Xu) holds. If not, rate measurements are interpreted under Hypothesis 4.2 alone, without the interpolation.

E4. Verify Algorithm 4 against brute-force reachability on small hypergraphs. Report both terminal-SCC agreement and all-SCC agreement; the framework's use of the terminal diagnostic (Choice B) is valid only if all-SCC computation is verified to be tractable at the scale used.

E5. Simulate at-least-once delivery with delays. Test three scenarios: (i) OR with causal stability alone, no (NR)/(WM); (ii) OR with (NR); (iii) OR with (WM); plus (iv) RW with (GF). The resurrection events predicted by the §6.1 negative example must appear in (i) and be absent in (ii) and (iii).

E6. Controversy: construct adversarial derivation sequences, including an attempt to launder a resolution by joining it with an earlier-epoch unresolved position and by asserting a non-increasing supersession delta. Verify 0/1.

