Appendix C — Counterexample Register

Each entry records an assumption that cannot be weakened, together with a concrete counterexample.

C-1 (Existential essentiality does not suffice for injectivity).
Let $S_O=\{a,b,w,d\}$ with rules $(b;a)$, $(a;w)$, $(a;d)$. The incidence graph is a tree, so (F1)–(F2′) of the earlier draft held. Under the existential definition $a\in\mathrm{Ess}$ (take $U=\{d\}$, target $w$), yet $a\in\mathrm{Cn}(\{b\})=\mathrm{Cn}(\mathrm{Core}_\prec(\{b\}))$, so the old Lemma 2.4 is false and $\Phi(\mathbf{1}_{\{a,b\}})=\Phi(\mathbf{1}_{\{b\}})$. Repair. Adopt the order-free definition (Def. 2.1); then Theorem 2.4 is immediate and the tree hypotheses are unnecessary.

C-2 (Acyclicity does not imply OR-Xu).
Let $S_O=\{p,q\}$ with symmetric rules $(q;p)$ and $(p;q)$. For $\prec_1$ with $p\prec_1 q$, $\mathrm{Atom}_{\prec_1}=\{p\}$; for $\prec_2$ with $q\prec_2 p$, $\mathrm{Atom}_{\prec_2}=\{q\}$. The implication hypergraph contains a directed cycle, so no acyclicity hypothesis can exclude this; Xu (2026, App. C) records the variant with three atoms. Repair. Adopt (OR-Xu) as a substantive hypothesis (Hyp. 2.5); do not attempt to derive it.

C-3 (Causal stability alone is insufficient for OR GC).
Replica $A$ adds tag $t_1$ for $n$; the add delta is delayed. $A$ removes $n$, the removal reaches every replica and is acknowledged, so it is causally stable. $A$ GCs the tombstone for $t_1$. A duplicate of the original add delta now arrives at $A$; since the tombstone is gone and $t_1\notin T_n$, the OR union re-adds $t_1$. Repair. Add (NR) (Hyp. 6.1) or (WM) (Hyp. 6.2); Prop. 6.3 is stated under one of these.

C-4 (Remove-wins suppression fails after GC of the generation floor).
If GC removes both the tombstone and the record of $g_{\mathrm{del}}(n)$, an add with generation $g\le g_{\mathrm{del}}(n)$ is no longer dominated. Repair. Adopt (GF) (Hyp. 6.5): the floor persists after tombstone GC.

C-5 (LWW join is not antisymmetric without a tie-breaker).
Let $\ell_1=(k,t,r,v_1)$ and $\ell_2=(k,t,r,v_2)$ with $v_1\neq v_2$. Then $\ell_1\preceq\ell_2$ and $\ell_2\preceq\ell_1$, so $\preceq$ is a preorder, not a partial order, and the join-semilattice theorem fails. Repair. Require per-key uniqueness of $(t,r)$ (stamp uniqueness as an invariant, not merely an optimization) or extend the order to $(t,r,\mathrm{val}(\ell))$ lexicographic.

C-6 (Laundering prevention is trivial on the flat stance lattice).
On $\mathcal{S}=\{0<\mathrm{challenged}<\mathrm{supported}<\mathrm{resolved}\}$ with join $=\max$, once any ancestor is $\mathrm{resolved}$, every join containing it is $\mathrm{resolved}$. The theorem then merely restates Rule 7.2. Repair. Use the product lattice $\mathbb{N}\times\mathcal{S}$ with the evidence threshold of Def. 7.1; Prop. 7.3 then excludes laundering by requiring an evidence-increasing supersession delta at epoch $e_\ast+1$.

C-7 (The "$H^1\neq 0$ iff gluing fails" claim is false).
Take the constant sheaf on a cycle graph. Then $H^1\neq 0$ (its dimension equals the cycle rank), but global sections exist and glue uniquely. Conversely, the sheaf axiom guarantees gluing never fails. Repair. Prop. 1.1 is one-directional: nonzero $H^1$ classes are exactly the 1-cochains not eliminable by vertex re-encoding, and the harmonic residual is the diagnostic. This is not a "gluing failure" statement.

C-8 (Fidelity is not a similarity score in the zero-denominator case).
If $\|N(x)\|=\|N(x')\|=0$, the ratio is undefined. Recording $\phi=0$ would conflate absence of information with maximal disagreement; recording $\phi=1$ would conflate it with exact agreement. Repair. Record $\bot$ in the provenance bundle and keep it distinct from $[0,1]$ throughout.

C-9 (The local block-sum rate conjecture is false).
The confusable-core rate is not $\sum_B H(\pi_{A_B})$ over confusable blocks; Xu (§III-C) exhibits jointly realizable cores whose rate exceeds any local decomposition. Repair. §4.6: state the rate as Körner graph entropy under pairwise realisability; §3.4: use the terminal-SCC diagnostic, which is a structural decomposition, not a rate formula.

C-10 (Terminal SCCs do not partition into confusable blocks).
In $a\leftrightarrow b,\ b\to c$, the confusable blocks are $\{a,b\}$ and $\{c\}$; the terminal SCCs are $\{c\}$ only. Any algorithm returning only terminal SCCs under-reports the partition. Repair. §3.4 Choice A (all-SCC; superlinear) or Choice B (terminal diagnostic, explicitly not the partition; correct only because §4.6 does not use the partition).

